:title("Java (subset) interpreter") ;
:description("Parses Java and executes it directly, without generating code. The
goal is the whole practical language; tests/java-test-full.java is the ratchet that
measures how far it is, and all 22 of its sections pass today.

Types: classes with instance and static fields (with initializers), instance,
static and abstract methods, OVERLOADED constructors with explicit this(...) and
super(...) chaining, static and instance initializer blocks (run in the order they
are written, before the constructor body), single inheritance with dynamic dispatch
and super.m() calls (a class must appear before its subclasses), records (canonical,
compact and secondary constructors, generated accessors and equals, hand written
accessors win), interfaces (constants, abstract methods, default and private
instance methods, static methods, and Iface.super.m() to pick one of two inherited
defaults), anonymous classes over a class or an interface, ENUMS (constructor
arguments, per-constant bodies as anonymous subclasses, values / valueOf /
ordinal / name / compareTo), NESTED, INNER and LOCAL classes (Outer.this and the
qualified o.new Inner(...) creation), and annotation types (parsed and dropped -
nothing reads them at run time).

Values: int / long / double arithmetic on one 32 bit-truncating number type,
boolean, String, arrays, null, and char as a boxed value that renders as its glyph
but compares and calculates as its code, so c == 65, 'b' - 'a' == 1 and
(char)(c + 1) == 'c' all hold. Literals cover hex / binary / octal / underscored /
suffixed integers, fractional, exponent and hex floats, char literals with the full
escape set (including the one-to-three digit octal form), and TEXT BLOCKS with
incidental-indent stripping, trailing-space stripping and the line-joining and
space-preserving escapes.

Statements and expressions: if/else, while, do-while, both for forms, the classic
colon switch and the arrow switch statement, switch EXPRESSIONS with yield in both
forms, labeled break and continue, try/catch/finally with TYPED and MULTI catch
clauses over a real java.lang exception hierarchy (Throwable down to the common
RuntimeExceptions, with getMessage), try-with-resources closing in reverse order,
throw with genuine unwinding, instanceof with a pattern binding, PATTERN SWITCH
(type patterns, case null, when guards and record DECONSTRUCTION patterns, which
instanceof takes too), enum constant labels, VARARGS (the call site collects the
trailing arguments, or forwards an array as-is), multidimensional and jagged
arrays with bare initializers and the C-style declarators, lambdas and method
references (static, bound, unbound and constructor) that become real values of the
functional interface they are assigned to, the full operator set including the
bitwise and shift operators with Java int semantics, and the compound assignments.

UNQUALIFIED calls work - check(x), not just this.check(x) - and pick their
receiver by Java's own rule: a method the enclosing class or an ancestor declares
static is called on the class descriptor, every other one on the enclosing
instance, and a static context has none, so the descriptor stands in there too.

Widened surface (parsed so a whole file is accepted): generics are parsed and
erased, call-site type arguments included; casts are identity except the primitive
ones, which truncate or box; implements / throws / permits clauses, TYPE
ANNOTATIONS in every position JLS 9.7.4 allows, qualified and generic supertypes,
several declarators per field, array class literals and the stray semicolon after
a type declaration are all parsed. -warn-unsupported downgrades what is left to a
warning so call graphs, CFGs and traces still work on partly understood files.

Unicode escapes are translated in PHASE ONE, before the scanner runs (JLS 3.3),
so a unicode escape anywhere in the file - in an identifier, in a keyword, in a
comment - is the character it denotes. An IMPLICITLY DECLARED class (JEP 512) is
accepted: a file that declares members but no class becomes a synthetic class
Main, whose `main` may be an instance method (the launcher constructs it).

Not genuinely modelled: generic type CHECKING (erased), sealed-hierarchy and
switch EXHAUSTIVENESS checking, method overloading by parameter type (constructors
are picked by arity), the flow scoping of pattern variables (a binding outlives the
test that introduced it), implicit FIELD access (a field is reached via this.x or
ClassName.x, the explicit style many code bases enforce - only calls resolve
unqualified), threads and reflection. A JPMS module declaration (module-info.java,
with requires / exports / opens / uses / provides, and 'import module M;') is PARSED
and dropped - nothing here has a module system for it to describe.

Fields live directly on the instance object, methods on the class descriptor.
main(String[] args) of class Main is the entry point; its return is void, so self
checking programs end with System.exit(failures).") ;


// --- main rules

// Shared token productions (languages/lib).
:include("lib/cstyle-comments.abnf") ;
:include("lib/ident-dollar.abnf") ;
:include("lib/dq-strings.abnf") ;
:include("lib/numbers.abnf") ;
:include("lib/bools.abnf") ;

:startRule(Program) ;
:whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// A stray ';' after a type declaration (`class F { };`) is legal and means
// nothing, at the top level and inside a class body alike.
Program     = UniPre [ Package ] { Import } { ModuleDecl | UnsupType | ClassDecl | RecordDecl | StraySemi } [ ImplicitClass ] ;
StraySemi   = ";" ;

// ----- JEP 512: an IMPLICITLY DECLARED class. A compilation unit that declares
// no class at all but does declare members is one class body without the
// `class X {` around it: `void main() { ... }` alone in a file is a program.
// The members are built into a synthetic class named Main, which is exactly the
// class this grammar's entry point looks for, and its `main` may be an INSTANCE
// method - the launcher instantiates the class when the static lookup misses.
// It is the LAST element of Program, so an ordinary file consumes everything
// through the declaration repetition and reaches it at EOF, where Member fails
// at once and the optional is skipped.
ImplicitClass <~~ buildClass(takeAll()) ~~>
            = ImplicitHead Member { Member } ;
ImplicitHead <~~ push("Main"); jEnterClass("Main") ~~>
            = NotTypeKw ;

// The implicit class may not START with a type declaration: a file whose first
// top-level item is `class` / `record` / `interface` / `enum` / `@interface` is
// an ordinary compilation unit, and if that declaration does not parse the file
// is broken - it is not a member of an implicit class. Without this, a record
// javac refuses (`record R(final int x) {}`) matched Method (`record` as the
// return type, `R(...)` as the signature, `{}` as the body) and five reject
// files were accepted for eight parse files gained; with it, the eight stay and
// the five come back. The modifier run is walked exactly as NoDupMods walks it.
NotTypeKw   = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var done = false
        var bad = false
        var rounds = 0
        while (!done && rounds < 64) {
            rounds++
            var moving = true
            while (moving) {
                var ch = c.peek(i)
                if (ch === 32 || ch === 9 || ch === 10 || ch === 13 || ch === 12) { i++ }
                else if (ch === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) { while (c.peek(i) >= 0 && c.peek(i) !== 10) { i++ } }
                else if (ch === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 42) {
                    i = i + 2
                    var g = 0
                    while (c.peek(i) >= 0 && g < 40000) {
                        if (c.peek(i) === 42 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) { i = i + 2; g = 40000 } else { i++; g++ }
                    }
                } else { moving = false }
            }
            if (c.peek(i) === 64) {            // an annotation, or `@interface`
                i++
                var ws = 0
                while (c.peek(i + ws) === 32 || c.peek(i + ws) === 9 || c.peek(i + ws) === 10 || c.peek(i + ws) === 13) { ws++ }
                var iw = "interface"
                var isAt = true
                for (var q = 0; q < iw.length; q++) { if (c.peek(i + ws + q) !== iw.charCodeAt(q)) { isAt = false } }
                if (isAt) { bad = true; done = true }
                else {
                    var an = 0
                    while (an < 4000) {
                        var ac = c.peek(i)
                        if ((ac >= 48 && ac <= 57) || (ac >= 65 && ac <= 90) || ac === 95 || ac === 36 || ac === 46 || (ac >= 97 && ac <= 122)) { i++; an++ } else { an = 4000 }
                    }
                    var ms = 0
                    while (c.peek(i + ms) === 32 || c.peek(i + ms) === 9 || c.peek(i + ms) === 10 || c.peek(i + ms) === 13) { ms++ }
                    if (c.peek(i + ms) === 40) {
                        i = i + ms + 1
                        var d = 1
                        var g3 = 0
                        while (d > 0 && g3 < 40000 && c.peek(i) >= 0) {
                            var pc = c.peek(i)
                            if (pc === 40) { d++ } else if (pc === 41) { d-- }
                            i++
                            g3++
                        }
                    }
                }
            } else {
                var n = 0
                var more = true
                while (more) {
                    var wc = c.peek(i + n)
                    if ((wc >= 97 && wc <= 122) || wc === 45) { n++ } else { more = false }
                    if (n > 12) { more = false }
                }
                var mods = ["public", "private", "protected", "static", "final",
                            "abstract", "synchronized", "native", "transient",
                            "volatile", "strictfp", "sealed", "non-sealed", "default"]
                var types = ["class", "record", "interface", "enum"]
                var hit = false
                for (var k = 0; k < mods.length; k++) {
                    var w = mods[k]
                    if (w.length === n) {
                        var ok = true
                        for (var j = 0; j < n; j++) { if (c.peek(i + j) !== w.charCodeAt(j)) { ok = false } }
                        if (ok) { hit = true }
                    }
                }
                for (var k2 = 0; k2 < types.length; k2++) {
                    var w2 = types[k2]
                    if (w2.length === n) {
                        var ok2 = true
                        for (var j2 = 0; j2 < n; j2++) { if (c.peek(i + j2) !== w2.charCodeAt(j2)) { ok2 = false } }
                        if (ok2) { bad = true }
                    }
                }
                if (hit && !bad) { i = i + n } else { done = true }
            }
        }
        return bad ? abnf.newToken("\x01type-declaration", 0) : undefined
    })()
~~) ;

// ----- Phase ONE of the Java compilation pipeline (JLS 3.3): a unicode escape
// is translated into the character it denotes BEFORE the scanner runs, so
// `public class X {}` really is `public class X {}` and a `\` really
// is a backslash the scanner then reads as an escape. It is a source PRE-PASS,
// not a grammar rule, and it is expressed as one: the script reads the whole
// source with c.getSrc(), rewrites it and hands it back with c.setSrc(), then
// restarts the parse position - the same shape as C's -pipe preprocessor stage,
// but inside one grammar so the corpus row measures it.
// A backslash is ELIGIBLE only when the run of backslashes ending at it has ODD
// length (JLS: an even number of preceding backslashes), so `"\\u0041"` stays
// the six characters A; the eligible one may then be followed by any
// number of `u` and must be followed by exactly four hex digits.
// The whole pass leaves after one indexOf on a file that contains no `\u`,
// which is every file but a handful.
UniPre      = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var src = c.getSrc()
        if (src.indexOf("\\u") < 0) { return undefined }
        var n = src.length
        var out = ""
        var last = 0      // start of the not-yet-copied run
        var i = 0
        var changed = false
        while (i < n) {
            if (src.charAt(i) !== "\\") { i++; continue }
            var j = i
            while (j < n && src.charAt(j) === "\\") { j++ }
            var odd = ((j - i) % 2) === 1
            if (odd && j < n && src.charAt(j) === "u") {
                var k = j
                while (k < n && src.charAt(k) === "u") { k++ }
                var hex = src.substring(k, k + 4)
                var ok = hex.length === 4
                for (var h = 0; h < hex.length; h++) {
                    var hc = hex.charCodeAt(h)
                    if (!((hc >= 48 && hc <= 57) || (hc >= 97 && hc <= 102) || (hc >= 65 && hc <= 70))) { ok = false }
                }
                if (ok) {
                    // Everything before the eligible backslash is copied as it
                    // stands - that includes the (j - i - 1) literal ones.
                    out += src.substring(last, j - 1)
                    out += String.fromCharCode(parseInt(hex, 16))
                    last = k + 4
                    i = k + 4
                    changed = true
                    continue
                }
            }
            i = j
        }
        if (!changed) { return undefined }
        out += src.substring(last, n)
        c.setSrc(out)
        c.setSdx(0)
        return undefined
    })()
~~) ;

// --- module-info.java: a JPMS module declaration (JLS 7.7). Modules are not
// modelled, and there is nothing in one to execute, so the whole declaration is
// pure syntax: no tag anywhere, so it leaves the stack exactly as it found it
// (the same treatment AnnotationDecl gets).
//
// `module`, `open`, `requires`, `transitive`, `exports`, `opens`, `to`, `uses`,
// `provides` and `with` are RESTRICTED keywords - ordinary identifiers
// everywhere else in the language - so each is matched as a bare literal HERE
// and is never added to ModKw or to any Kw* rule. A file that is not a module
// declaration cannot reach this rule's body: it is an ordered alternative that
// only commits once `[open] module <name> {` has matched, and every other
// top-level form fails at `module`.
ModuleDecl  = LeadMods [ "open" KwEnd ] "module" KwEnd ModName "{" { ModDirective } "}" ;
// A module / package / service name is dotted, and nothing consumes it, so this
// is QualName without QualName's push.
ModName     = Id { "." Id } ;
ModDirective = ModRequires | ModExports | ModOpens | ModUses | ModProvides | StraySemi ;
ModRequires = "requires" KwEnd { ReqMod } ModName ";" ;
ReqMod      = ( "transitive" | "static" ) KwEnd ;
ModExports  = "exports" KwEnd ModName [ ModTo ] ";" ;
ModOpens    = "opens" KwEnd ModName [ ModTo ] ";" ;
ModTo       = "to" KwEnd ModName { "," ModName } ;
ModUses     = "uses" KwEnd ModName ";" ;
ModProvides = "provides" KwEnd ModName "with" KwEnd ModName { "," ModName } ";" ;

// An optional package header (parsed and ignored) then repeated imports. Each
// import path is resolved against the runtime builtins (core.stdlibImports); one
// that does not resolve aborts, unless -warn-imports downgrades it to a warning
// (see resolveImport in interp-core.js). 'import static a.b.C.d' resolves the
// dotted path too. QualName also accepts a trailing '.*' wildcard. Neither Package
// nor Import leaves a value on the stack.
// package-info.java carries its annotations on the package declaration.
Package     <~~ pop() ~~> = LeadMods KwPackage QualName ";" ;
// `import module java.base;` (JEP 476) imports every package a module exports.
// It is the SECOND alternative on purpose: `module` is a restricted keyword, so
// `import module.foo.Bar;` is still a legal ordinary import of a package called
// `module`, and trying the plain form first keeps that reading. Either
// alternative leaves exactly one name on the stack for resolveImport.
Import      <~~ resolveImport(pop(), up.pos) ~~>
            = KwImport [ "static" KwEnd ] QualName ";"
            | KwImport "module" KwEnd QualName ";" ;
QualName    <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> = Id { "." Id } [ "." "*" ] ;

ClassDecl   <~~ buildClass(takeAll()) ~~>
            = [ LeadMods ] KwClass Id <~~ push(up.in); jEnterClass(up.in) ~~> [ SkipAngle ]
              [ KwExtends ExtRef ]
              [ ImplementsSkip ] [ Permits ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
// A supertype may be qualified and generic (`extends Outer.Super`, `extends
// A<B>.C`). The type arguments are dropped and the dotted name is kept whole -
// jResolveClass tries it, then falls back to the last segment.
ExtRef      <~~ push({ext: jJoinDots(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = { Annotation } Id <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] { "." { Annotation } Id <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] } ;

// record Pair(int a, int b) { } -- generates fields, a constructor and accessors.
RecordDecl  <~~ buildRecord(takeAll()) ~~>
            = [ LeadMods ] KwRecord Id <~~ push(up.in); jEnterClass(up.in) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] RParams
              [ ImplementsSkip ] [ Permits ] "{" { RMember } "}" ;
// A record body is restricted (JLS 8.10.4): no INSTANCE initializer
// (instance.initializer.not.allowed.in.records) and no instance FIELD
// (record.cannot.declare.instance.fields). Both are simply absent here -
// InstInit is gone, and a field must carry `static`, which HasStatic demands.
// An interface has no instance initializer (initializer.not.allowed, JLS 9.1.1):
// InstInit is simply absent from its member set.
IMember     = StaticInit | NestedType | Ctor | AbstractMethod | Method
            | CompactCtor | Field | StraySemi ;
RMember     = StaticInit | NestedType | Ctor | AbstractMethod | Method
            | CompactCtor | RField | StraySemi ;
RField      <~~ pushFields(takeAll()) ~~>
            = HasStatic Mods FType FieldVar { "," FieldVar } ";" ;
RParams     <~~ push({rparams: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "(" [ RParam { "," RParam } ] ")" ;
// A record component may carry annotations and may be VARARGS
// (`record R(String name, String... rest) {}`, JLS 8.10.1). The `...` is parsed
// and dropped - the component is bound to whatever single argument arrives.
RParam      = { Annotation } Type { Annotation } [ "..." ] Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ;

// A member starting with '{' (or 'static {') can only be an initializer block, so
// both come first and no other member alternative is disturbed.
Member      = StaticInit | InstInit | NestedType | Ctor | AbstractMethod | Method
            | CompactCtor | Field | StraySemi ;
// static { ... } runs once when the class is built; { ... } runs on every
// construction, in TEXTUAL order with the field initializers around it.
StaticInit  <~~ push({kind: "sinit", body: pop()}) ~~>
            = "static" KwEnd Block ;
InstInit    <~~ push({kind: "iinit", body: pop()}) ~~>
            = Block ;

// A constructor. Its body is NOT a plain Block: an explicit this(...) / super(...)
// invocation may only stand first, and it decides what initializes the object, so it
// is parsed as a separate head rather than as an ordinary statement.
Ctor        <~~ push(makeCtor(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LeadMods ] [ SkipAngle ] Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ParamList [ Throws ] CtorBody ;
CtorBody    <~~ push(makeCtorBody(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" [ CtorCall ] { Statement } "}" ;
// A record's COMPACT constructor: no parameter list at all - it receives the record
// components, normalizes them, and the canonical assignment happens afterwards.
CompactCtor <~~ push(makeCompactCtor(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LeadMods ] Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> Block ;
// An abstract (or interface) method: a signature and no body.
AbstractMethod <~~ push({kind: "amethod", name: pop()}) ~~>
            = Mods [ SkipAngle ] Type Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ParamList [ CDims ] [ Throws ] ";" ;

Method      <~~ push(makeMethod(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Mods [ SkipAngle ] Type Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ParamList [ CDims ] [ Throws ] Block ;

// One `int a, b = 2, c;` declares several fields, which share their modifiers
// and type but not their initializer - so each declarator becomes its own member.
Field       <~~ pushFields(takeAll()) ~~>
            = Mods FType FieldVar { "," FieldVar } ";" ;
FieldVar    <~~ push(makeFieldVar(takeAll())) ~~>
            = NotResvKw NotUscore Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ CDims ] [ "=" VarInit ] ;

// Modifiers before a member: annotations (with or without arguments) are parsed and
// ignored; ModWord keeps the modifier text so `static` is still detectable.
Mods        <~~ push({mods: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = NoDupMods { Annotation | ModWord } ;
ModWord     <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
            = ModKw ;
FType       <~~ push({ty: up.in}) ~~>
            = NotVarKw Type ;

// A parameter may carry annotations, `final` and varargs (`...`); all ignored.
ParamList   <~~ push(makeParams(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ParamOk "(" [ Param { "," Param } ] ")" ;
// A trailing `T... name` is a VARARGS parameter: the call site collects the
// remaining arguments into an array (jFixVarargs), unless it already passes one.
// RecvParam is the JLS 8.4.1 RECEIVER parameter (`void m(Foo Foo.this)`,
// `Inner(Outer Outer.this)`) - a declaration of the type of `this`, not a real
// parameter, so it pushes nothing and makeParams never sees it. It has to come
// FIRST: the ordinary form would match `Foo Foo` and then die on `.this`.
Param       = RecvParam | { Annotation | "final" KwEnd } Type { Annotation } [ VarArgs ] Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ CDims ] ;
RecvParam   = { Annotation } Type { Id "." } "this" KwEnd ;
VarArgs     <~~ push({vararg: true}) ~~>
            = "..." ;
// The C-style declarator suffix: `int a[]` and `int[] a[]` declare arrays too.
CDims       = { Annotation } "[" "]" { { Annotation } "[" "]" } ;

// Types are parsed and (apart from field defaults) ignored. Generic arguments
// (<...>), dotted names, extra array dimensions and the extra primitive keywords are
// accepted and dropped. Semantics stay dynamic.
// A TYPE ANNOTATION may sit in front of the type, in front of any segment of a
// qualified one, and in front of each array bracket pair (`@A String @A []`,
// `Outer.@A Inner`) - JLS 9.7.4. They are dropped like every other annotation.
Type        = { Annotation } ( PrimName KwEnd | "String" KwEnd | "void" KwEnd | "var" KwEnd NoBracket
              | NotResvKw NotVarArr Id [ SkipAngle ] { "." { Annotation } Id [ SkipAngle ] } ) { { Annotation } "[" "]" } ;
PrimName    = "int" | "boolean" | "char" | "long" | "double" | "float" | "short" | "byte" ;

// Every statement production pushes exactly one thunk: function() -> undefined | signal.
Statement   = Block | If | While | Do | ForEach | For | Switch | Return | Yield | Break | Continue
            | Try | Throw | Assert | Synchronized | LocalClass | Labeled | Decl | Empty | ExprStmt ;
// A LOCAL class: the declaration builds it where the file is walked (so it is
// registered before the enclosing method ever runs) and leaves a do-nothing
// statement behind. Its scope is wider than Java's - it stays visible after the
// method - which no program that compiles under javac can observe.
LocalClass  <~~ push(function() {}) ~~>
            = NoSealed NestedType ;
// yield: the value of the enclosing switch EXPRESSION arm. It travels as its own
// signal, so it passes out of nested blocks and loops like return does.
Yield       <~~ push(makeYield(pop())) ~~>
            = "yield" KwEnd Expression ";" ;

Block       <~~ push(makeBlockStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" { Statement } "}" ;

// JLS 14.5: the body of an if / loop / labeled statement is a *Statement*, and a
// local variable declaration and a local class declaration are NOT Statements -
// they are BlockStatements, which only a block admits. `if (true) int x = 0;` and
// `if (true) class X {}` are what javac rejects with variable.not.allowed /
// class.not.allowed, and SubStmt is exactly Statement minus those two.
SubStmt     = Block | If | While | Do | ForEach | For | Switch | Return | Yield | Break | Continue
            | Try | Throw | Assert | Synchronized | Labeled | Empty | ExprStmt ;

If          <~~ push(makeIf(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwIf "(" Expression ")" SubStmt [ KwElse SubStmt ] ;

While       <~~ push(makeWhile(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = KwWhile "(" Expression ")" SubStmt ;

Do          <~~ push(makeDo(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = KwDo SubStmt KwWhile "(" Expression ")" ";" ;

// The classic colon switch (on int or String): execution starts at the first matching
// label and runs until a break or the end of the switch. Labels may stack.
Switch      <~~ push(makeSwitch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwSwitch "(" Expression ")" "{" { SwitchCase } "}" ;
// The arrow forms (case 1, 2 -> body) are sugar for stacked classic labels
// whose shared body ends in an implicit break: no fallthrough, several labels
// per arm. Tried first; they fail fast at ':' for the classic forms.
SwitchCase  = ArrowCase | ArrowDefault | Case | Default ;
ArrowCase   <~~ pushArrowCase(takeAll()) ~~>
            = KwCase NotDfltKw CaseLabel { "," CaseLabel } "->" ArrowBody ;
ArrowBody   = Block | Throw | ExprStmt ;
ArrowDefault <~~ pushArrowDefault(takeAll()) ~~>
            = KwDefault "->" ArrowBody ;
// Stacked labels on ONE colon case - `case 3, 4:` (JLS 14.11) - become one
// classic case object per label: all but the last have an empty body, so they
// fall through into it, which is exactly what Java's stacked labels mean.
Case        <~~ pushColonCase(takeAll()) ~~>
            = KwCase NotDfltKw CaseLabel { "," CaseLabel } ":" { Statement } ;

// ----- Case labels (shared by the statement switch and the switch expression).
// A label is a PATTERN (type or record deconstruction, with an optional `when`
// guard), `null`, a literal, a plain name, or any other constant expression.
// The specific forms come first: a bare name would otherwise be swallowed by
// Lambda ("case S -> 1" parses as the lambda "S -> 1" under a full expression),
// and a name is also the only shape an ENUM constant label has - it names no
// variable in any scope, so it is compared by its source TEXT instead.
CaseLabel   <~~ push({lab: makeLabel(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = ( NullLabel | RecPat | TypePat | LitLabel | NameLabel | ExprLabel ) [ WhenGuard ] ;
NullLabel   <~~ push({lnull: true}) ~~>
            = "null" KwEnd ;
LitLabel    <~~ push({lval: pop()}) ~~>
            = LabelLit LabelEnd ;
LabelLit    = TextBlock | JavaStr | CharLit | JNum | True | False ;
// Every numeric literal in the language goes through NumOk first, so a literal
// javac's scanner refuses never reaches the parser.
JNum        = NumOk ( HexLit | BinLit | OctLit | FloatLit | UscoreInt | SuffInt | IntLit ) ;
NameLabel   <~~ push(makeNameLabel(takeAll(), stripWs(up.in))) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "." Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } LabelEnd ;
// A constant-expression label is a whole expression - `case "a" + "b":`,
// `case cond ? "x" : "y":` - so the literal and name forms, which are tried
// first, must decline whenever the label does NOT end right here. Without the
// guard the shorter reading wins, the case then dies on the leftover `+` / `?`,
